<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aosta Valley</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a> Valley is a mountainous region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and drained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dora-baltea">Dora Baltea</a>. Its passes, the Great and Little St Bernard, made it a key route across the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>; the Salassi held it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conquered them in 25 BCE and founded Augusta Praetoria, modern Aosta. It is now an autonomous region with French and Italian as official languages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aosta-valley.jpg" alt="Aosta Valley" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hagai Agmon-Snir حچاي اچمون-سنير חגי אגמון-שניר · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CastelloDiF%C3%A9nisJuly292023_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
